● Yes — there's a clean path, and it reuses the architecture we already trust. I thought through the failure modes before answering, because the obvious designs break on MM2's two hard constraints (no negation, and
  patterns can't reach inside nested subterms). Here is the assessment.

  The core design: a second decompose/rebuild pass over each derivative

  Simplification can't be done by rewriting (* 1 x) "wherever it appears" — an MM2 pattern only matches a whole fact, never a subterm at arbitrary depth. But our engine already solved that shape of problem once:
  decompose top-down, rebuild bottom-up. A simplifier is a structural sibling of the differentiator, run after (zz out):

  1. Seed: for each (result $e $de), put the derivative tree $de into its own todo namespace (stodo/ssub, separate from todo/sub so the differentiator's rules don't re-fire on derivative subterms).
  2. Decompose the derivative with the same driver pattern, recording its own (slevel l) facts for the sweep count.
  3. Base cases: (s x x) from (dvar x), (s a a) from (const a), and (s n n) for integer leaves via the same pure-sink parse gate we built last time — the leaf problem is solved identically.
  4. Rebuild bottom-up, where (s E S) means "E simplifies to S": for a node (op u v) with simplified children su, sv, build the simplified parent — and clean redundancy at the root only, which is sufficient because
     children are already clean when the parent is assembled. Each level's redundancy is eliminated before the level above ever sees it, so one bottom-up pass fully reduces the tree.
  5. Collect: join (result $e $de) with (s $de $sd) and replace the result fact (the -/+ sinks).

  On your example the pass proceeds: (* 1 x) → x, (* x 1) → x, (* 0 x) → 0, (* 2 1) → 2; then (+ 0 2) → 2; final result (+ (+ x x) 2). (Going further to (+ (* 2 x) 2) needs a collection rule e + e → 2·e — easy to add,
  but a scope decision.)

  The one genuinely hard part, and its workaround

  The rebuild rules overlap: "if su is 0, parent is sv" versus the default "parent is (op su sv)". The default is an else-branch — "no special case applied" — which is negation, and MM2 can't express it. Naively
  writing both produces junk variants that multiply up the tree (I traced this failure mode before ruling the naive design out).

  The workaround is to implement the else-branch by sequencing plus deletion instead of negation. Assembly writes an intermediate fact (sraw E candidate) that parents never read. Then, in strict tag order within each
  sweep:

  - special-case rules fire first: (sraw $e (+ 0 $x)) → delete the sraw, write (s $e $x); likewise for (+ $x 0), (* 1 $x), (* $x 1), (* 0 $x), (* $x 0), (- $x 0), (/ 0 $x), (/ $x 1), (pow $x 1), (pow $x 0), (neg 0),
    and an integer-folding rule fed by a pure exec (sum_i64/product_i64) so (+ 2 3) becomes 5;
  - a finalize rule fires last: any sraw still standing survived every special case, so it is the default — convert it to (s E candidate) unconditionally.

  Whatever the special cases deleted, the finalizer never sees. That is an else-branch built from execution order. The overlapping special cases are confluent (e.g. (* 0 1) reduces to 0 by either rule), so firing
  order among them doesn't matter. And because parents read only s, never sraw, the re-assembly that happens on later sweeps (saturation re-derives facts) converges to the same clean facts instead of leaking dirty
  variants — this was the subtle bug in my first two designs, and the sraw/s namespace split is what kills it.

  Alternatives I considered and rejected

  - Simplify during differentiation (make each brule emit simplified output directly): the product/quotient/power rules build multi-level trees in one template, so redundancy appears nested within a single step where
    root-cleanup can't reach it. Fixing that means splitting every derivative rule into single-level steps — a rewrite of the whole engine for no gain.
  - Post-process outside MM2 (Python/Rust over the dumped space): trivial, but abandons the goal of doing it in MM2.

